  {
    "episode_id": 95428,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/5/95428.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 55,
    "turn_text": "That's an interesting point. I think a lot of people would say that anytime you're doing an industrial or economic type of policy, worker conditions, worker rights should be a part of that. All the way to a jobs guarantee. I take your point. Maybe that doesn't belong in a climate plan. But as far as I know, Inslee did not include that in his climate plan. I thought on the detail, it was interesting to see the way that Inslee laid this out. That's probably where his experience as a governor comes into play. Washington State passed and Inslee signed into law a 100% clean energy bill by 2045. Notably, that's one decade later than his national plan. His state would get there after the fact. That might have been the sunrise movement pressure moving up the deadline.",
